"""Long-term memory for your agents, from Alexein.

Generated by `alexein-cli init openai-agents` (alexein-cli {version}); yours to edit.
The settings are here; recall, remember and the tools come from `alexein.openai_agents`
(https://docs.alexein.ai/integrations/openai-agents).

    agent = Agent(name="Assistant", tools=memory.tools())  # write=True: and save
    result = await Runner.run(
        agent,
        message,
        context=MemoryContext(user_id="dana", session_id="conv-7"),
        run_config=RunConfig(call_model_input_filter=memory.recall),
    )
    await memory.remember(result)  # for run_streamed, once its events are consumed

`memory.recall` adds what memory holds to the instructions, never to your history.
Whose memory is read is your code's choice, never the model's: any context with a
`user_id` will do. The key is ALEXEIN_API_KEY and the address ALEXEIN_API_URL, read on
the first call: importing needs neither.
"""

from alexein.openai_agents import AlexeinMemory, MemoryContext

__all__ = ["MemoryContext", "memory"]

# True skips search's relevance check: free and quicker, a little less precise. Use it
# where a reply cannot wait, like a voice agent's turn.
FAST = {fast}

# False keeps your agent running while memory is down: a turn goes on without its
# memory, a save is skipped, a tool tells the model memory is unavailable, and a
# warning is logged on the `alexein` logger. True raises instead. A wrong key raises
# either way: that is a mistake to fix.
STRICT = False

# How much of what memory holds goes into the instructions, in characters.
MAX_CHARS = 4000

# Seconds each attempt at a call may take; a call is tried up to three times.
TIMEOUT = 10.0

memory = AlexeinMemory(
    fast=FAST,
    max_chars=MAX_CHARS,
    strict=STRICT,
    timeout=TIMEOUT,
)
